Kam Kreiser 2026-09-14T08:29:27+10:00
KS-780: normaliseOrgId lives ONCE, in @secuura/shared — the revoke policy's private copy is gone and originate re-exports the shared one

Two behaviourally identical normalisers sat in two layers: `services/originate/src/services/orgId.ts` (the two originate resolvers and the documents route) and a private `normOrgId` inside `packages/shared/src/security/keyRevokePolicy.ts` (the organisation arm of API-key revocation, a destructive surface). Neither could witness the other drifting.

The move:
  - NEW `packages/shared/src/security/orgId.ts` — the one implementation (trim-then-lowercase; blank and non-string are null), exported from `packages/shared/src/index.ts`.
  - `keyRevokePolicy.ts` — `normOrgId` deleted; the org arm's two call sites call `normaliseOrgId` from `./orgId`; the docblock that argued for the copy now records the move.
  - `services/originate/src/services/orgId.ts` — `export { normaliseOrgId } from '@secuura/shared';` with the docblock rewritten: its old reason for staying service-local ("nothing outside it compares organisation ids this way") stopped being true when the policy landed. `provenance.ts`, `gdprService.ts` and `routes/documents.ts` are untouched — their import lines are byte-identical and their blobs asserted unchanged.
  - Definitions census (git grep -P, untracked included): exactly ONE `normaliseOrgId`/`normOrgId` definition under packages/ + services/ (control at the parent head: 2).

Cells:
  - `packages/shared/src/__tests__/ks780-normalise-org-id-one-implementation.test.ts` — P1 trim · P2 case · P3 blank-is-absent · P4 non-string-is-absent · S1 the package export IS the module function · S2 the policy's source carries no private normaliser and imports the shared one, calling it twice · S3 the policy really goes through it (an upper-cased claim on a padded key is `same-organisation`).
  - `services/originate/src/__tests__/ks780-org-id-is-the-shared-implementation.test.ts` — I1 the re-export IS the shared function (identity) · I2 the module's source is a re-export with no private definition · I3 the properties hold through it.
  - The fixture uuid carries hex LETTERS on purpose: the first draft used an all-digit uuid, which upper-cases to itself, and the red-proof below left P2/S3/I3 green — a case cell that cannot fail. Measured, then fixed.

ONE deliberate hunk outside the move's own files, ruled YES by Wednesday (22:26Z): `services/originate/src/__tests__/ks695-erasure-by-external-ref.test.ts` mocks `@secuura/shared` by listing its exports by hand, so after the re-export `gdprService.ts` read `normaliseOrgId is not a function` on the 11 cells that reach the subject-org comparison. Added one pass-through line — `normaliseOrgId: jest.requireActual('@secuura/shared').normaliseOrgId,` — the REAL function, not a stub (a stub there would be the second implementation this removes). Nothing else in that file changed.

Red-proof, the ticket's own done-means (one character in the shared implementation — `.toLowerCase()` dropped — packages/shared rebuilt, three packages run):
  packages/shared      2 failed | 833 passed (835)   P2, S3
  services/security    1 failed | 204 passed (205)   ks764 policy: "organisation ids compare NORMALISED"
  services/originate   6 failed | 595 passed (601)   ks597-b (d) lower-case claim vs UPPER-case · ks597-issuer-org-bind case-only difference · ks695 UPPERCASE organizationId claim (through the pass-through mock) · ks780 I3 · qa-f4 caller-claim case · qa-f4 stored-value case
  Restored by content (whole-file sha asserted), rebuilt (dist sha identical), green: shared 43 files 835/835 · security 16 files 205/205 · originate 57 suites 601/601 · api-gateway 34 files 343/343 · tsc --noEmit clean in shared, security, originate.

STACKED on PR #799's current head 6da848891 (the policy file this edits is #799's); merge order #799 then this. Behaviour pinned unchanged.

